OS-9/68K FREEWARE COLLECTION  --  start here===========================================Community software for OS-9/68000, with whatever documentation and sourcecould be found for each program and a record of where it came from.  Someof it has source here; much of it has none that survived.Most programs need no runtime module at all.  The rest are starred inDOC/INDEX because they use Microware's cio, and cio, csl, csl020, math andmath881 are on this disk by Microware's permission, so nothing has to befetched.TO TRY SOMETHING: DOC/START-HERE is fifteen programs that run with nothingset up -- no arguments, no data to find, no terminal type to get right --and says how to get back out of each.TO FIND SOMETHING: DOC/CATEGORIES groups every program by what it is for --shells, editors, games, archivers -- which is the order to read in beforeyou know the names.  DOC/INDEX is the alphabetical list and says more abouteach one.  Both are generated from the disk itself.OS-9 itself is Microware's, and still a current product.  This is the softwarethe community wrote for it.------------------------------------------------------------------STARTING IT------------------------------------------------------------------ON YOUR OWN OS-9 SYSTEM.  This is a data disk for an OS-9 you alreadyhave; there is no OS9Boot on it and it boots nothing.  Give it a disk ofits own, reached by two device names: /dd, where most programs here lookfor their data, and /h0, which a few of them carry inside instead.  Yourown system disk keeps its own name and is mounted as /h1, which is whereSYS/login looks: it puts /h1/CMDS on the path, so runb, your shell, r68and l68 are found by the programs that want them.That way round, and not the other way.  Measured over the 1034 programshere: 479 look for their own data at /dd and 55 want data at /h0, nearlynine to one.  Leave your own system on /dd and most of this collection cannotfind itself.    bash /dd/SYS/loginfrom your own shell gives a session with PATH, HOME, TERM and TERMCAPset; or run the programs directly, with those set as SYS/login shows.To get the collection onto that disk, write the raw RBF image whole toa disk your system mounts.  We have not done this on hardwareourselves; DOC/README-RUNNING says what is known.WITH os9exec, a community emulator that builds on macOS, Linux, Windowsand most anything with a C compiler, to try it first.  Its devices arenamed by environment variables: the image as both OS9DISK and OS9H0, andyour own OS-9 system or SDK as OS9H1 if you have one:    OS9DISK=/absolute/path/osk-freeware.dd  \    OS9H0=/absolute/path/osk-freeware.dd    \    OS9H1=<your OS-9>  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginWith OS9H1 set, the programs that want runb or Microware's shell findthem.  Everything on this disk was tested this way.    TYPE COMMANDS BY NAME.  SYS/login sets PATH across every program    directory, so this is all you need:        cookie        soundex </dd/SYS/motd        advent                           (after: cd /dd/GAMES/ADV)    Under os9exec, run the DISK IMAGE.  File permissions and record    locking work on an RBF image as OS-9 expects, and on a host directory    they do not; os9exec's own `mount -k' makes a blank image for work of    your own.------------------------------------------------------------------AS A SINGLE DISK IMAGE------------------------------------------------------------------    This collection also ships as one RBF disk image, with room left free    for score files, saves and your own work.        OS9DISK=/path/to/image  OS9H0=/path/to/image  \        OS9H1=<your OS-9>  os9exec /dd/CMDS/bash    The image carries proper OS-9 owner and permission bits, which a host    directory cannot: every module is e+pe+r+pr, every data file r+pr with    no execute.  On a host directory those bits are whatever your Mac or    Linux box happened to have, which is meaningless here.    NAME THE IMAGE WHAT YOU LIKE when you give OS9DISK its full path:    `OS9DISK=/somewhere/freeware.rbf' mounts perfectly.  The filename    only matters when os9exec has to FIND the image for itself -- a    secondary device set with OS9H1 and no path, say -- and then it    wants `dd' or `h0'..`hz'.  Measured 2026-08-29; the note here said    the name was required outright, and it is not.------------------------------------------------------------------WHERE TO LOOK------------------------------------------------------------------    DOC/STATUS        what runs, what needs cio, what does not run.  START HERE    DOC/INDEX         one line saying what every program IS    DOC/ORIGINS       one line saying where every program CAME FROM    DOC/README-DOCS   the four ways to find out how to use something    DOC/README-RUNNING  deployment, mtools, DOS disks, GNU chess    DOC/EFFO-INFO     the authors' own descriptions AND licence conditions    DOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT   math.l for any 68k, math881.l for a 68881,                               and fpu if you build with Ultra C    DOC/README-BUSERR   why a bus error is usually the wrong terminal,                        not a broken program    DOC/README-MODULES  the files in CMDS that are not commands    SOURCES.txt       provenance, build method, and what was left out and why    DOC/<program>/    per-program documentation    SRC/<package>/    source, where it survived------------------------------------------------------------------WHAT IS ON IT------------------------------------------------------------------    CMDS/, CMDS/GAMES/   the commands and the games                   (including cio, math, math881, csl and csl020 --                    Microware's runtime modules, here by permission)    CMDS/REBUILT/   alternates, other editions and other-CPU builds    CMDS/GCC139/, CMDS/GCC2/          GNU C 1.39 and 2.x    SBPROLOG/      SB-Prolog 2.2 with its whole module library    SRC/, DOC/     source where it survived, and documentation    DOC/CATEGORIES lists every program grouped by what it is for, with a    count for each group.  It is generated from the disk, so it cannot go    stale; a list typed out here would, and had.------------------------------------------------------------------LICENCE------------------------------------------------------------------    Public domain and freeware throughout.  SEVEN programs carry a condition    their authors asked for -- "no military use", or "peaceful    applications" only, which is the same ask in the author's own words:    disktest, help, hexed, hist, i_am_i, rxmod and txmod.  One of them,    the help system, is by os9exec's own author.  An eighth, space,    carries conditions of its own and `help space' has them.  All are    named in DOC/EFFO-INFO and marked in DOC/INDEX.  Please pass the    conditions on with the software.    The only Microware files here are the five runtime modules named above,    included with their permission; SOURCES.txt records the exchange.  An    earlier CMDS/NONFREE directory is gone: of the six binaries in it, four    turned out not to be Microware's at all and are back in CMDS, and two    were theirs and were removed.